r polydipsia disfavor autoimmune beta cell destruction?", "result_summary": "Lack of classic Type 1 diabetes symptoms weakens but does not completely refute autoimmune beta cell destruction as a potential mechanism.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_against", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 6, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal temperature and lack of wasting signs disfavor hypermetabolic syndrome?", "result_summary": "Normal thermoregulation and absence of weight loss argues against hypermetabolic endocrine syndrome despite severe hyperglycemia.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "moderate_against", "B4": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 7, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the 'painful rash' more consistent with an autoimmune phenomenon than insulin resistance?", "result_summary": "Painful rashes suggest active inflammatory processes, which are more characteristically autoimmune than insulin resistance-associated skin changes.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_for", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "moderate_against"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.16660436488444821, "B2": 0.3939240020464378, "B3": 0.06785293815153953, "B4": 0.24556301426271446, "B5": 0.12605568065486009, "B1.1": 0.052063864026390065, "B1.2": 0.041651091221112053, "B1.3": 0.031238318415834042, "B1.4": 0.041651091221112053, "B2.1": 0.1969620010232189, "B2.2": 0.11817720061393133, "B2.3": 0.07878480040928755}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "Patient has no immediate airway compromise, respiratory failure, hemodynamic instability, or altered mental status requiring urgent airway intervention or stabilisation", "required_actions": ["Monitor blood glucose, consider lab work for DKA/ketones", "Order C-peptide/INSA for tumor/inflammatory etiologies", "CT imaging for neoplastic causes", "Thyroid antibody panel for APS II"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch satisfies posterior >= 0.75, multiple dangerous alternatives (B1.2: adrenal carcinoma, B1.1: glucagonoma) remain unresolved despite evidence against them, and requestable discriminators (CT imaging, hormone panels) are still available to refine differentials"}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.9, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.24556301426271446, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 55-year-old man presents to his primary care physician with fatigue, malaise, and a painful rash. The patient states that his symptoms have been worsening over the past week. He also has experienced abdominal pain and diarrhea. He has a medical history of obesity and smokes regularly. His temperature is 97.5°F (36.4°C), blood pressure is 142/82 mmHg, pulse is 85/min, respirations are 15/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for an erythematous rash with papules and plaques on the patient's face, torso, and extremities. He states that the rash is painful. A fingerstick blood glucose is unable to accurately read his blood glucose and gives a reading of > 500 mg/dL.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta cell tumor"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Hypercortisolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Insulin resistance"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: fatigue, malaise, and a painful rash", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms worsening over the past week", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms: abdominal pain and diarrhea", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: obesity", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Social history: regular smoking", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Temperature: 97.5°F (36.4°C)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Blood pressure: 142/82 mmHg", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pulse: 85/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Respirations: 15/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Oxygen saturation: 98% on room air", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam finding: erythematous rash with papules and plaques on face, torso, and extremities", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E12", "kind": "direct", "content": "Patient states the rash is painful", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E13", "kind": "direct", "content": "Fingerstick blood glucose result: > 500 mg/dL (reading unable to be accurate)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Insulin receptor antibody positivity', 'Insulin resistance'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=30%, source=primekg]\n\nHypermetabolic Endocrine Syndrome vs Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n\nHypermetabolic Endocrine Syndrome vs Autoimmune Insulin Receptor Syndrome (Type B Diabetes):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: arthralgia/arthritis, autoimmune antibody positivity, autoimmunity, fasting hypoglycemia, increased circulating antibody level\n\nAlpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma) vs Autoimmune Insulin Receptor Syndrome (Type B Diabetes):\n  Favours first:  abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n  Favours second: arthralgia/arthritis, autoimmune antibody positivity, autoimmunity, fasting hypoglycemia, increased circulating antibody level\n\nRelated to Hypermetabolic Endocrine Syndrome: congenital nervous system disorder, multiple congenital anomalies/dysmorphic syndrome-intellectual disability, polyendocrinopathy\n\nRelated to Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma): malignant leydig cell tumor, malignant testicular leydig cell tumor, sex cord-stromal benign neoplasm\n\nRelated to Autoimmune Insulin Receptor Syndrome (Type B Diabetes): autoimmune disease of endocrine system, hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  He also has experienced abdominal pain and diarrhea → feeding difficulties → Hypermetabolic Endocrine Syndrome\n  He also has experienced abdominal pain and diarrhea → feeding difficulties → Hypermetabolic Endocrine Syndrome"}
