{"case_id": "MB_13", "timestep": 5, "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man presents to his primary care physician with fatigue, malaise, and a painful rash. The patient states that his symptoms have been worsening over the past week. He also has experienced abdominal pain and diarrhea. He has a medical history of obesity and smokes regularly. His temperature is 97.5°F (36.4°C), blood pressure is 142/82 mmHg, pulse is 85/min, respirations are 15/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for an erythematous rash with papules and plaques on the patient's face, torso, and extremities. He states that the rash is painful. A fingerstick blood glucose is unable to accurately read his blood glucose and gives a reading of > 500 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?\n\nOptions:\nA. Alpha cell tumor\nB. Beta cell destruction\nC. Beta cell tumor\nD. Hypercortisolism\nE. Insulin resistance\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Multisystem Syndrome with Widespread Painful Erythematous Rash, Diarrhea, and Suspected Endocrine-Immune Cross-Mechanism Aetiology", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.45, "supporting_facts": ["Progressive symptoms over one week (fatigue, malaise, rash deterioration)", "Widespread erythematous rash with papules/plaques and systemic GI symptoms", "Objective evidence of severe hyperglycemia (>500 mg/dL)", "Risk factors for metabolic syndrome (obesity, smoking)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute Viral Exanthem: No evidence of infectious prodrome or viral markers", "Chronic Autoimmune Vasculitis: Incompatible with 7-day symptom evolution", "Isolated Adverse Drug Reaction: No medication history mentioned despite multisystem presentation", "Malignancy-Related Paraneoplastic Syndrome: Could be considered but lacks constitutional wasting signs"], "alarm_features": ["Fingerstick glucose >500 mg/dL"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Neop